  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A strong system tonight through Saturday is \nexpected to bring widespread rain with some snow over the north \ntonight and strong winds tonight into Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThe low pressure system is centered to the west over Quebec and\nwill continue to track ENE. As of this update the warm front \nhas stretched over the St John Valley, which has brought temp \nabove freezing across the region. The next round of rain ahead \nof the approaching cold front will continue to move from W to E \nthrough the rest of the afternoon. For tonight, the temps will \ncontinue to warm, keeping all remaining precip as rain. After \nsunset, but before midnight, the cold front will move through \nthe region, gradually dropping temps into the 30s as NW winds \nincrease. For Saturday, the remaining shortwave energy behind \nthe front should create some showers in the north. Any showers \non the back side of the cold front will be in the form of \nisolated snow showers in the North Woods.\n\nIn addition, the approaching high pressure behind the exiting\nlow will tighten pressure gradients on Saturday. Winds gust are\nexpected to reach up to 35 mph, creating possible isolated power\noutages.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Generally dry weather Sunday through late next\nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure will generally control the weather Sunday through\nlate next week. Several frontal passages will occur during that\ntime. However, with only limited moisture available expect that\nthe frontal passages will generally be dry.",
